    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A Deccan dynasty that ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Vatapi</a>, modern Badami in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, from about 543 until 753.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulakeshin-ii">Pulakeshin II</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmada-river">Narmada</a> and ruled from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a>.</li><li>Its temples at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aihole">Aihole</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pattadakal">Pattadakal</a> mark the move from rock-cut caves to free-standing stone temples.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aihole">Aihole</a> inscription of 634 names the poets <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalidasa">Kalidasa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharavi">Bharavi</a>, giving one of the earliest firm dates by which Kalidasa’s works were already famous.</p></aside>
    </section>

