    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pulakeshin-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pulakeshin-ii.jpg" alt="Pulakeshin II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pulakeshin-ii">Pulakeshin II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pulakeshin_statue,_badami.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a> rose in the mid-sixth century in the valley of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaprabha-river">Malaprabha River</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, a region previously dominated by the Kadamba dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banavasi">Banavasi</a>. Their origins are obscure: later dynastic legends gave them a northern or divine ancestry, but most historians regard them as a local Kannada-speaking family that had served the Kadambas before asserting its independence. The founder of the dynasty’s power was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulakeshin-i">Pulakeshin I</a>, who, according to a rock inscription at Badami dated 543, fortified the hill of Vatapi, the modern town of Badami, and who performed the <em>ashvamedha</em>, or horse sacrifice, to proclaim his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirtivarman-i">Kirtivarman I</a> (r. c. 566–597) defeated the Kadambas, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nala-dynasty">Nalas</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauryas-of-konkan">Mauryas of the Konkan</a>, turning a local principality into the leading power of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>. Kirtivarman’s brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangalesha">Mangalesha</a> (r. c. 597–610) defeated the Kalachuris of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahishmati">Mahishmati</a> and took Revatidvipa, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a>, and an inscription of 578 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishnavism">Vaishnava</a> cave at Badami records that he had it excavated. Mangalesha’s attempt to pass the throne to his own son provoked a civil war in which his nephew, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulakeshin-ii">Pulakeshin II</a>, killed him and seized the crown around 610, opening the most expansive period in the dynasty’s history.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/badami"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/badami.jpg" alt="Badami" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BadamiCaves87.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sanyam Bahga, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukya</a> kingdom rested on the agriculture of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-plateau">Deccan plateau</a> and the valleys of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaprabha-river">Malaprabha</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tungabhadra-river">Tungabhadra</a> rivers, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millets</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulses">pulses</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and, on irrigated land, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> were grown. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">Copper</a>-plate and stone charters record villages granted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> and temples, a practice that extended settled cultivation and bound local communities to royal patronage. Towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aihole">Aihole</a>, whose name later became attached to one of the great merchant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, linked the interior to the ports of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konkan-coast">Konkan coast</a>, through which trade with western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> passed. Religious life was plural. The kings presented themselves as patrons of Vedic sacrifice and of the gods <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, yet they also supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a>: the Meguti temple at Aihole, which carries the inscription of 634, is a Jain shrine, and one of the four cave temples at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a> is Jain. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> was the language of court poetry and of most royal charters, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> appeared increasingly in inscriptions, including the verse inscription of Kappe Arabhatta at Badami, usually dated to about 700 and counted among the earliest examples of Kannada poetry. The Chinese pilgrim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a>, who passed through the kingdom around 641, described its people as proud, honest, grateful for kindness and quick to avenge a wrong, and remarked on their love of learning.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xuanzang.jpg" alt="Xuanzang" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xuanzang_w.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> were above all a military dynasty, and their power depended on infantry, cavalry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a> raised both by the royal household and by feudatory chiefs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> reported that the king kept a body of champions and many war elephants that were given wine before battle, and that his neighbours feared him. The greatest victory came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulakeshin-ii">Pulakeshin II</a>, who halted the southward advance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a>, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a>, on the line of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmada-river">Narmada River</a>. The date is debated, with scholars proposing either about 618–619 or the 630s, but the outcome fixed the Narmada as the frontier between the powers of the north and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> for generations. Pulakeshin II also used a fleet, said in his inscription to number hundreds of ships, to take Puri, the island capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauryas-of-konkan">Konkan Mauryas</a>, and around 624 he conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vengi">Vengi</a> in the eastern Deccan. His wars with the Pallava dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a> began a rivalry that lasted for over a century. In 642 the Pallava king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narasimhavarman-i">Narasimhavarman I</a> defeated him, occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Vatapi</a> and took the title <em>Vatapikonda</em>, ‘conqueror of Vatapi’; Pulakeshin II apparently died in the fighting. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramaditya-i">Vikramaditya I</a> expelled the Pallavas by about 655 and raided Kanchipuram in return, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramaditya-ii">Vikramaditya II</a> occupied Kanchipuram around 740. In about 739 the Chalukya governor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avanijanashraya-pulakeshin">Avanijanashraya Pulakeshin</a>, repulsed an Arab raid from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty.jpg" alt="Chalukya dynasty" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukya dynasty</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chalukyas_at_their_height.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Map created from DEMIS Mapserver, which are public domain. Koba-chan. Reference: [1] Borders by Rawn3012, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukya</a> kings bore titles such as <em>Satyashraya</em>, ‘refuge of truth’, <em>Prithvivallabha</em>, ‘lord of the earth’, and <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharajadhiraja">Maharajadhiraja</a></em>, and their emblem was the boar, the form in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> raised the earth from the waters. Royal legitimacy rested on Vedic ritual, on lavish grants to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> and temples and on the claim to protect the social order. The core territory around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Vatapi</a> was ruled directly through provinces and districts known as <em>vishaya</em>, but much of the kingdom was held through subordinate dynasties such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ganga-dynasty">Western Ganga dynasty</a>, the Alupa dynasty and the Sendrakas, who paid tribute and supplied troops. Princes of the royal house governed distant conquests as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroys</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulakeshin-ii">Pulakeshin II</a> installed his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kubja-vishnuvardhana">Kubja Vishnuvardhana</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vengi">Vengi</a>, where he founded the line of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-chalukyas">Eastern Chalukyas</a> around 624, and his son Dharashraya Jayasimha governed southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, founding another branch. These arrangements extended Chalukya power quickly but also created semi-independent rulers whose loyalty depended on the strength of the king at Vatapi. Succession was a recurring weakness: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangalesha">Mangalesha</a>’s death in civil war around 610 and the thirteen-year interregnum after 642, during which Pulakeshin II’s sons contested the inheritance, both showed how fragile the throne could be. Royal women were prominent patrons; the queen Vijayabhattarika issued grants in her own name, and queens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramaditya-ii">Vikramaditya II</a> built the principal temples at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pattadakal">Pattadakal</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lad-khan-temple"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lad-khan-temple.jpg" alt="Lad Khan Temple" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lad-khan-temple">Lad Khan Temple</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lad_NKAD90.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Meesanjay, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The most lasting legacy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a> is architectural. Within two centuries their craftsmen moved from the rock-cut caves of Badami, excavated in the late sixth century, to free-standing stone temples at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aihole">Aihole</a>, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lad-khan-temple">Lad Khan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durga">Durga</a> temples, and then to the mature complex at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pattadakal">Pattadakal</a>. There the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virupaksha-temple-pattadakal">Virupaksha</a> temple, built around 740 by Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lokamahadevi">Lokamahadevi</a> to commemorate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramaditya-ii">Vikramaditya II</a>’s victory over the Pallavas, stands beside temples in the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bednur">Nagara</a> style, making Pattadakal a meeting point of the two great traditions of Indian temple building; the site was inscribed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1987. Chalukya builders drew on the Pallava <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kailasanathar-temple-kanchipuram">Kailasanatha</a> temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a>, and their work in turn shaped the Rashtrakuta dynasty, whose rock-cut <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kailasa-temple-ellora">Kailasa temple</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ellora-caves">Ellora</a> is often compared with the Virupaksha. The Aihole inscription of 634, composed by the poet Ravikirti, names the poets <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalidasa">Kalidasa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharavi">Bharavi</a> and so provides a firm latest date for both. Politically, the dynasty made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> a lasting counterweight to the kingdoms of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a> and drew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> country into the mainstream of Indian statecraft. Its name outlived it: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-chalukyas">Eastern Chalukyas</a> ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vengi">Vengi</a> until the eleventh century, and the Western Chalukyas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basavakalyan">Kalyani</a>, who rose in 973, claimed descent from the house of Vatapi.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/dantidurga"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dantidurga.jpg" alt="Dantidurga" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/dantidurga">Dantidurga</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rashtrakutas_Coins_25-07-23_15-44-48-445.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Shraman revival, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom remained strong under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayapida">Vinayaditya</a> (r. c. 681–696) and Vijayaditya, whose long reign to about 733 was largely peaceful and rich in temple building, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramaditya-ii">Vikramaditya II</a> (r. c. 733–744) carried its arms once more to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a>. Yet more than a century of warfare against the Pallavas had stretched its resources, and the system of feudatory rule left powerful subordinates in command of troops and revenue in the north of the kingdom. Among them was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dantidurga">Dantidurga</a> of the Rashtrakuta dynasty, who held territory around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ellora-caves">Ellora</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berar">Berar</a> and who built up his own power in the 740s, apparently while still acknowledging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukya</a> overlordship; his inscriptions claim victories in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> and Kanchi. Around 753 Dantidurga defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirtivarman-ii">Kirtivarman II</a> and assumed imperial titles, and the Chalukya king seems to have survived for a few more years with a reduced domain in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> before Dantidurga’s uncle and successor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> I, completed the conquest by about 757. The end was a transfer of power rather than a collapse of society: the Rashtrakutas took over the Chalukya territories, administration and feudatory networks and became the dominant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> power for the next two centuries, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manyakheta">Manyakheta</a> from the early ninth century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a> itself declined to a provincial town, but its temples and inscriptions remained, and in 973 a Chalukya line restored the dynasty’s name at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basavakalyan">Kalyani</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a> made the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> a major power for two centuries, halting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha of Kannauj</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmada-river">Narmada</a>, founding branch dynasties in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vengi">Vengi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, and creating at Badami, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aihole">Aihole</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pattadakal">Pattadakal</a> the early stone temples that shaped later architecture in southern and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</em></p>

